<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tu scendi dalle stelle</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1744">1744</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;Tu scendi dalle stelle&quot; (&quot;You Come Down from the Stars&quot;) is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas-carol">Christmas carol</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, written in 1744 by Alphonsus Liguori in the musical style of a pastorale. Liguori was a prominent Neapolitan priest and scholastic philosopher, later canonised, who founded the Redemptorist missionary order. He wrote both the melody and the original words.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Alphonsus Liguori wrote both the melody and the original words, while staying at the Convent of the Consolation, a house of his order at Deliceto in the province of Foggia. The Italian carol, titled &quot;Little song to Child Jesus&quot;, followed an earlier song in Neapolitan, &quot;Quanno nascette Ninno&quot;, which is sometimes called the &quot;Carol of the Bagpipers&quot;. The Italian carol became widely popular in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. Though sung in many arrangements, it is traditionally associated with the zampogna, the large Italian bagpipe.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The text addresses the infant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> as the King of Heaven who comes down from the stars to a cave, in cold and frost. The first stanza calls him a trembling baby and asks how much it cost him to have loved the singer. The second describes the Creator of the world as lacking clothes and fire, and says that his poverty makes the singer love him the more, since love made him poor. Several English translations exist, some in the style of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-james-version">King James Bible</a>, as well as a literal English rendering of the Neapolitan song.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The earliest known sound recording was made by the baritone Rodolfo Fornari, a member of the Boston Opera Company, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> on 21 September 1915 under the title &quot;Novena Di Natale&quot;, and was issued in 1916 by Columbia. On 24 September 1917 the tenor Pasquale Feis recorded it for Victor as &quot;Pastorale Di Natale&quot;; the Library of Congress preserves that recording. A version published in 1932 by A. Paolilli's Music Co. of Providence, Rhode Island, credits the music to Tommaso Capocci and the words to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-ix">Pope Pius IX</a>, without mentioning the earlier work.</p><p>Robertino Loretti recorded it in 1961, and Sergio Franchi in 1965. The bilingual song &quot;O Bambino (One Cold and Blessed Winter)&quot;, composed by Anthony Velona with words by Remo Capra, takes its melody directly from the carol and was first recorded by the Harry Simeone Chorale in 1964. Domenico Scarlatti used the tune in a sonata, and Ottorino Respighi used it in the second movement of his Trittico botticelliano of 1927.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tu scendi dalle stelle” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
